.page jobcontrol 7 stopping, continuing and prioritising processes

# DESCRIPTION
A process can be stopped and continued. A stopped process keeps its memory and its open files but none of its threads runs, and `ps` shows the state `T` for it. The kernel half of this is complete; what a shell builds on it (`jobs`, `fg`, `bg`) is described in gapsh(1).

# SIGNALS
@ *STOP*
    Stops the process. It cannot be caught, blocked or ignored.
@ *TSTP*
    What Ctrl-Z sends to the foreground job. A program may catch or ignore it; by default it stops.
@ *TTIN*, *TTOU*
    Sent by the terminal to a background job that tries to read from it, or (when the terminal has `tostop` set, or for changing terminal settings) to write to it. By default they stop the job.
@ *CONT*
    Continues a stopped process, even if the process blocks or ignores it. If it has a handler for `CONT`, that runs too.

`kill -STOP 123` and `kill -CONT 123` do this by hand. A parent is told with `CHLD` when a child stops or continues, unless it asked for `SA_NOCLDSTOP`, and `waitpid` with `WUNTRACED` or `WCONTINUED` reports it once.

# TERMINALS
A terminal belongs to a session and has a foreground process group. Only that group may read from the terminal: a background process that tries gets `TTIN` and stops, or the error `EIO` if it ignores `TTIN`. The shell moves jobs in and out of the foreground (`tcsetpgrp`); a job that stopped because it wanted the terminal goes on by itself once its group is in front. If a process group has lost every parent that could continue it (it is orphaned) and holds a stopped process, the kernel sends the group `HUP` and then `CONT`, so nothing stays stopped forever. Orphaned groups ignore `TSTP`, `TTIN` and `TTOU`.

# PRIORITIES
Every process has a nice value from -20 (greedy) to 19 (polite), 0 by default, inherited by children and kept across `exec`. It sets the length of the time slice the scheduler gives to the threads of the process: when several programs want the processor, a polite one gets a smaller share. Only root may lower a nice value. See nice(1) and renice(1); `ps -l` lists the values.

# SEE ALSO
kill(1), ps(1), nice(1), renice(1), gapsh(1), proc(5)
